  "title": "A single fastest way grows one ring at a time, and only along a line",
  "status": "Proved",
  "claim": "On tori of rule-60 rings with equally spaced law-(12,12) contacts: (1) inserting one ring into the twist torus <(2,1),(N,0)>, a local rewiring that cuts the three contacts spanning the gap and adds five, gives <(2,1),(N+1,0)>, so growth by single-ring insertion from 4 rings keeps every contact persistent (a half-turn at every contact) and the plain first need on time at every stage; likewise the triangular torus <(1,-1),(N,0)> from 3 rings (four contacts cut, seven added) and the ladder <(0,2),(n,t)> grown by rungs; (2) every sequence of on-time square, triangular or honeycomb tori of unbounded index eventually holds a ladder, twist or sqrt 3 vector, so its ring balls hold at most 4r + 1 rings: growth that keeps the plain reading through tori grows a line, not a plane.",
